From: Danny Lu <suricata.lu@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 29/29] efi_loader: tcg2: support 5 PCR banks and index our own
 algorithm list

Two defects in lib/efi_loader/efi_tcg2.c stop EFI_TCG2_PROTOCOL from
registering on the Advantech ECU-150v2, whose TPM is an STMicroelectronics
part (VendorID 0x104a, DeviceID 0x0003) on I2C2 @ 0x2e.

Observed on hardware before this patch:

    ** Unable to read file ubootefi.var **        <- pre-existing, harmless
    Failed to load EFI variables                 <- pre-existing, harmless
    Failed to delete final events config table
    Cannot install EFI_TCG2_PROTOCOL
    Error: Cannot initialize UEFI sub-system, r = 7

r = 7 is EFI_DEVICE_ERROR. efi_tcg2_register() reaches
efi_init_event_log() -> create_specid_event(), whose ret is initialised to
EFI_DEVICE_ERROR and left untouched by both of its "goto out" paths, so any
failure inside it surfaces exactly like this. "Failed to delete final events
config table" narrows it further: that line only comes from tcg2_uninit(),
which efi_tcg2_register() calls solely on the efi_init_event_log() /
efi_add_protocol() / efi_create_event() failures, not on the earlier
platform_get_tpm2_device() or tpm_startup() ones.

1) hash_algo_list[] has four entries, but the count check claims five

   MAX_HASH_COUNT is ARRAY_SIZE(hash_algo_list), which lists SHA1, SHA256,
   SHA384 and SHA512 -- four. tpm2_get_pcr_info() nonetheless says:

       /*
        * We only support 5 algorithms for now so check against that
        * instead of TPM2_NUM_PCR_BANKS
        */
       if (pcrs.count > MAX_HASH_COUNT || pcrs.count < 1)
               goto out;

   The comment and the array disagree; upstream's list does carry a fifth
   entry, SM3_256 (see include/tpm-v2.h in current U-Boot, where the list was
   later moved). This patch adds it, so MAX_HASH_COUNT becomes 5 and matches
   the documented intent.

   The ECU-150v2's TPM reports five banks. Read back at the U-Boot prompt with
   "tpm2 get_capability 0x5 0 0x50000000 1 ; md.b 0x50000000 0x20":

       00 00 00 05                 count = 5
       00 04 03 00 00 00           SHA1     (0x0004), not allocated
       00 0b 03 ff ff ff           SHA256   (0x000b), all 24 PCRs allocated
       00 0c 03 ff ff ff           SHA384   (0x000c), all 24 PCRs allocated
       00 27 03 00 00 00           SHA3_256 (0x0027), not allocated
       00 28 03 00 00 00           SHA3_384 (0x0028), not allocated

   5 > 4, so the check rejected a perfectly ordinary TPM.

   SM3_256 is only ever added to the table of algorithms this code knows how
   to name; nothing hashes with it unless a TPM reports an active SM3 bank.
   On this board active_pcr is SHA256|SHA384 -- alg_to_mask() returns 0 for
   the two SHA3 banks, which tpm2_get_pcr_info() already handles by logging
   "Unknown algorithm" and skipping them.

2) create_specid_event() bounds its loop with the TPM's bank count

       for (i = 0; i < pcr_count; i++) {
               u16 hash_alg = hash_algo_list[i].hash_alg;

   pcr_count is what the TPM reported, but the array being indexed is our own
   list; every other loop over hash_algo_list[] in this file correctly uses
   MAX_HASH_COUNT. Two consequences:

     - With pcr_count > ARRAY_SIZE(hash_algo_list) it reads past the array.
       Only the check in (1) keeps that from happening, which is why a
       one-line list extension would otherwise be load-bearing for memory
       safety rather than for functionality.

     - With pcr_count smaller than our list it silently skips algorithms. A
       TPM reporting one bank (SHA256 only) would examine hash_algo_list[0],
       i.e. SHA1, find nothing active, leave alg_count at 0, and fail the
       "number_of_algorithms < 1" check below -- the same EFI_DEVICE_ERROR,
       for the opposite reason.

   Using MAX_HASH_COUNT makes the loop mean "which of the algorithms we know
   about are active on this TPM", which is what the surrounding code expects,
   and makes the result independent of how many banks the part reports. This
   half is not needed for the ECU-150v2 once (1) is in place; it is here so a
   different TPM part does not reintroduce the same failure.

Why this matters for EVE: pkg/pillar/evetpm/tpm.go:1247-1251 refuses to seal
the vault key while PCR 0 or PCR 4 is all-zero in the SHA-256 bank, and those
PCRs are extended by exactly this protocol. Without it VaultStatus never
reports ConversionComplete, volumemgr blocks in wait.WaitForVault
(pkg/pillar/cmd/volumemgr/volumemgr.go:253), and every app instance stays in
INITIAL with no error recorded anywhere.

Upstream-Status: Backport-ish [(1) mirrors upstream's algorithm list; (2) is
equivalent to how upstream later restructured the loop]

Signed-off-by: Danny Lu <suricata.lu@gmail.com>
---
diff -urN a/lib/efi_loader/efi_tcg2.c b/lib/efi_loader/efi_tcg2.c
--- a/lib/efi_loader/efi_tcg2.c
+++ b/lib/efi_loader/efi_tcg2.c
@@ -95,6 +95,11 @@
 		EFI_TCG2_BOOT_HASH_ALG_SHA512,
 		TPM2_SHA512_DIGEST_SIZE,
 	},
+	{
+		TPM2_ALG_SM3_256,
+		EFI_TCG2_BOOT_HASH_ALG_SM3_256,
+		TPM2_SM3_256_DIGEST_SIZE,
+	},
 };
 
 struct variable_info {
@@ -1624,7 +1629,7 @@
 	if (err)
 		goto out;
 
-	for (i = 0; i < pcr_count; i++) {
+	for (i = 0; i < MAX_HASH_COUNT; i++) {
 		u16 hash_alg = hash_algo_list[i].hash_alg;
 		u16 hash_len = hash_algo_list[i].hash_len;
 
